# The shipped marine material set.
#
# This file is the definition, not a copy of one -- there is no material table in
# any header and no enumeration in any shader. `gpu::MaterialLibrary::load` reads
# it, the fragment shader indexes the storage buffer built from it, and a mod adds
# a surface by loading a second file of exactly this form afterwards. A repeated
# name overrides, so a mod can also restate one of these.
#
#   material NAME
#       base_colour  r g b     linear reflectance, each within [0, 1]
#       roughness    x         perceptual, within [0.03, 1]; alpha = x^2
#       metalness    x         within [0, 1]; 0 dielectric, 1 conductor
#       opacity      x         optional, default 1 (carried, not yet rendered)
#
# base_colour, roughness and metalness are all required: a block that stops short
# is rejected rather than completed with defaults.
#
# Values are linear reflectances, not sRGB swatches. Paint over steel is a
# dielectric however metallic the substrate is -- only the bare and blasted
# surfaces are conductors, and that distinction is most of what makes a ship's
# topside read as painted rather than as raw metal.

# Marine topcoat over primer, the light grey most of a hull is actually painted.
# Semi-gloss enamel: bright, but far from a mirror.
material painted_steel_topside
    base_colour 0.68 0.70 0.72
    roughness   0.35
    metalness   0.00

# The band at the waterline, between the antifouling and the topside. Dark, and
# rougher than the topside because it lives in the splash zone.
material boot_topping
    base_colour 0.045 0.040 0.042
    roughness   0.42
    metalness   0.00

# Cuprous-oxide antifouling. Matt by design -- a self-polishing coating is
# ablating, so it never holds a gloss.
material antifouling
    base_colour 0.26 0.065 0.045
    roughness   0.72
    metalness   0.00

# Blasted or mill-scale steel, the only genuinely metallic surface here. F0 is
# the base colour rather than 0.04, which is what makes it read as metal.
material bare_steel
    base_colour 0.56 0.57 0.58
    roughness   0.40
    metalness   1.00

# Rust is iron oxide, and oxide is an insulator. Metalness 0 is not an
# approximation here: corroded steel stops behaving like a conductor optically,
# which is why rust looks like dirt rather than like tarnish.
material rusted_steel
    base_colour 0.30 0.12 0.055
    roughness   0.92
    metalness   0.00

# Holystoned timber deck. Wide, diffuse, and light enough to matter to the
# ambient term.
material timber_deck
    base_colour 0.50 0.39 0.24
    roughness   0.60
    metalness   0.00

# Wheelhouse glazing, seen from outside. Opacity is recorded but the pass does
# not yet honour it (no transparency sort, no refraction), so this renders as the
# very smooth dark dielectric that glass is when it is reflecting the sky.
material glass
    base_colour 0.030 0.035 0.040
    roughness   0.05
    metalness   0.00
    opacity     0.12

# The sea, so it goes through the same pipeline as the hull and therefore shares
# the depth buffer rather than being composited beside it.
#
# **This base colour is deliberately above the physical one.** Real water has an
# albedo of two or three per cent and takes almost all of its apparent brightness
# from the sky reflected in it plus subsurface scattering, and neither exists yet;
# at 0.02 the sea renders very nearly black, which is correct and useless. Five
# per cent stands in for the missing terms. It comes back down when the reflection
# work lands -- and being a number in a file rather than a constant in a shader is
# the point of it living here.
material sea_water
    base_colour 0.050 0.150 0.210
    roughness   0.09
    metalness   0.00

# The plating around a hole. Steel that was inside the plate a moment ago: no
# paint, no mill scale, and no time to oxidise -- brighter and sharper than
# `bare_steel`, which is a weathered blasted surface rather than a fresh fracture.
# A conductor, like every unpainted steel surface here.
#
# `gpu::HullPaint::tornEdge` names this and `gpu::SceneMesh::appendShip` resolves
# it only for a damaged ship, so a `.materials` file that predates it still paints
# an intact one. A mod restates the block to change what a tear looks like.
material torn_plate_edge
    base_colour 0.62 0.62 0.63
    roughness   0.28
    metalness   1.00
